              width={1100}
              format="webp"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              alt="A weathered steel shipping container sitting directly on gravel at an active construction site, its cargo doors facing the camera at ground level, with a pickup truck, stacked lumber, scaffolding and a crew in high visibility clothing behind a chain link fence."
            />
            <figcaption>
              <b>This is what Section B means.</b> Corrugated steel, sat flat on the gravel, doors at
              ground level so a forklift can drive straight in. It is a different product from the
              boxes in Section A, and a quote for one tells you nothing about the other. That is
              Question 02.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <div class="dir-grid">
            {publishedConexProviders.map((p) => (
              <article class="dir-card">
                <h4 class="dir-name dir-name-lg">
                  <a href={p.url} rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">{p.name}</a>
                </h4>
                <dl class="dir-dl">
                  {p.facts.map((f) => (
                    <div>
                      <dt>{f.k}</dt>
                      <dd>
                        {f.parts.map((part) => (
                          part.chip ? <span class="dir-chip">{part.text}</span> : <Fragment>{part.text}</Fragment>
                        ))}
                        {f.source && <span class="dir-src">{f.source}</span>}
                      </dd>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </dl>
                <a class="dir-link" href={p.url} rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">{p.linkLabel} →</a>
              </article>
            ))}
          </div>

          <div class="dir-callout">
            <span class="m">The thing worth knowing</span>
            <p>Nearly every company in this region that rents real shipping containers also sells them.</p>
            <p class="dir-callout-body">
              That is not a criticism. It is a normal and sensible way to run a container yard: the
              same steel earns either way, and plenty of these companies are very good at both. But it
              changes how you should make the call. If you phone around for conex rental in Ohio,
              Indiana, or Kentucky, expect most of the yards you reach to do both, and expect the
              conversation to be able to go either direction. That is fine as long as you know which
              one you are in. Ask up front (that is Question 08 above), and make sure the written
              quote says rental or purchase on it.
            </p>
            <p class="dir-callout-body">
              It also explains why this section is {conexCountWord} names long instead of twenty. We
              only listed companies we could verify rent without also selling, because sending you to
              a competitor's sales page while telling you we were helping you rent would make this
              whole page worthless.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p class="dir-verify">
            Every company on this page was checked on its own website on {VERIFIED_LABEL} · Rates
            shown are the companies' own published figures, quoted as published · No paid placements
            · No brokers or lead-generation sites · If something here is out of date, tell us and we
            will fix it
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. CLOSING CTA -->
